WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.930
They put an elephant in a dark room, and everyone who walks in reaches out a hand.

2
00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:13.220
One touches the trunk and says "this is a waterspout," another touches the ear and says "no, it's a fan" — and not one of them is lying.

3
00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:18.950
So does this story say "everyone is right in their own truth," or exactly the opposite?

4
00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.840
They had brought an elephant from India.

5
00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:35.570
Nobody in the town knew what an elephant was; not one person had even seen a picture of one.

6
00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:40.660
They put the animal in a dark house and let the curious crowd in, one at a time.

7
00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:43.670
Inside, the eye is useless.

8
00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:50.200
You have a hand, you have a palm, and in front of you there is an enormous something, breathing.

9
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:55.290
The first man goes in, stretches out his hand, touches the trunk.

10
00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:59.580
He comes back out and reports: this animal is like a roof gutter.

11
00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:04.190
Long, thick, it curls, and it blows air from its tip.

12
00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:08.320
The second goes in, and his hand meets the ear.

13
00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:13.890
He comes out and reports as well: no, it is nothing like a gutter, this is a fan.

14
00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:17.380
Broad, thin, it sways.

15
00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:26.150
The third finds the leg: what are you all talking about, this is a straight upright post, like a marble column.

16
00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:32.040
The fourth lays his hand against the back: a flat surface like a throne, wide and firm.

17
00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:34.570
Not one of the four is lying.

18
00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.900
All four describe accurately what they touched.

19
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:42.030
And all four are at each other's throats.

20
00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:48.080
The man who wrote the story in this form was Mevlânâ Celâleddîn-i Rûmî, in the thirteenth century.

21
00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:51.250
He tells it in the third volume of the Masnavi.

22
00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.100
But the story is older than he is.

23
00:01:54.100 --> 00:02:05.590
A century earlier, Sanai stages the same scene in his work The Walled Garden of Truth, and there the townspeople are blind; no one has seen the elephant, because no one has eyes.

24
00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:17.880
Let us go back further still: in a narrative found in the Buddhist Udana, the Collection of Inspired Utterances, the king of Savatthi gathers together people blind from birth, has an elephant brought before them,

25
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:22.810
lets each of them touch a different part, and then asks: what is an elephant?

26
00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:35.420
The one holding the head says "it is a pot," the one holding the tail says "it is a broom," the one holding the ear says "it is a winnowing basket." Then they turn on one another; the account says the men came to blows.

27
00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:45.470
The same elephant wanders through Indian thought as well, especially in the Jain doctrine of many-sidedness, where the lesson is that no single account can encompass the whole of reality.

28
00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:57.600
In the nineteenth century, through John Godfrey Saxe's poem, The Blind Men and the Elephant, the story crosses over to the West, and today it has become a parable that everyone in the English-speaking world knows.

29
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.410
Now notice the small but shattering change Rumi makes.

30
00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:04.420
In Sanai, the people are blind.

31
00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:08.710
Rumi erases the blindness and puts darkness in its place.

32
00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:11.400
The eyes are intact.

33
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.730
The flaw is not in us, it is in the room.

34
00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:19.820
And this is not a detail; it is a move that changes the entire meaning of the story.

35
00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:24.590
Because blindness is a fate, whereas darkness is a condition.

36
00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:30.160
For a blind man there is nothing to be done; for a man in the dark, there is something to be done.

37
00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.490
We will come to what that difference is.

38
00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:39.220
But first, let us see this: we walk into that room ourselves, every day.

39
00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:41.430
Think of a traffic accident.

40
00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:44.920
Four witnesses, four statements.

41
00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:52.570
One says "the car was going very fast," because he heard the sound and when he turned to look, the damage was severe.

42
00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:58.940
Another says "it was slow, but it braked suddenly," because she was standing right beside it.

43
00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:02.750
One swears the light was red, another that it was amber.

44
00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.920
These people are not counted as liars in court.

45
00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:09.570
Each of them has taken hold of some part of the elephant.

46
00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:13.060
Or take a closer example: family.

47
00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:18.310
Two siblings who grew up in the same house describe their childhood thirty years later.

48
00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:23.720
One remembers the father as harsh and distant, the other as self-sacrificing and quiet.

49
00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:25.610
Both are honest.

50
00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:35.820
Both have touched a different part of the same man; one was the first child and came along during the father's exhausted years, the other was the last child and grew up with a man who had softened.

51
00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:39.470
What happens when these two memories collide at the dinner table?

52
00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:42.960
Nobody says "we are both right" and feels relieved.

53
00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.090
Each of them feels the other has stolen their past.

54
00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:50.580
And this is exactly where the story leaves us.

55
00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:52.630
What we touched is real.

56
00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:55.480
What we reported is honest.

57
00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.650
But the descriptions do not match.

58
00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:08.060
And at this point a very tempting sentence comes to mind, a sentence that looks like a rescue: "Everyone has their own truth." That sentence appears to end the quarrel.

59
00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:13.310
It calls no one a liar, it belittles no one, it brings peace to the table.

60
00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:16.960
Now we are going to test how well that sentence holds up.

61
00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:25.570
Because the first time it was uttered, roughly twenty-five hundred years ago, by a thinker on the Aegean coast, people noticed that there was a trap underneath it.

62
00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:28.580
The man was Protagoras of Abdera.

63
00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:35.910
He lived in the fifth century before Christ, and he was among the most expensive and most sought-after teachers in Athens.

64
00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:45.480
The sentence that has come down to us from him is this: man is the measure of all things; of the things that are, that they are, and of the things that are not, that they are not.

65
00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.970
That sentence sounds modest, but it is not.

66
00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:55.180
What Protagoras is saying is this: there is no independent bench that decides how a thing is.

67
00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:58.350
The measure is the one who measures.

68
00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:05.520
Plato, while discussing this view in the dialogue Theaetetus, gives a very everyday example: the wind is blowing.

69
00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:08.850
We are both standing in the same wind.

70
00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:12.340
I feel cold, you do not.

71
00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:15.190
Now tell me: is the wind cold?

72
00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:18.040
Protagoras's answer is clear.

73
00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:21.530
The wind is cold for me, and not cold for you.

74
00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:33.820
And beyond that, there is no referee we can turn to and ask "how many degrees is it, really"; or if there is one, we can reach it only through someone's senses, someone's measuring instrument, someone's way of looking.

75
00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:38.110
Let us concede this much: it is not a foolish view.

76
00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:44.800
On the contrary, it sounds like the most honest summary of what those four men in the dark room actually experienced.

77
00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:48.290
Everyone speaks by the measure of their own palm.

78
00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:55.300
But Plato puts one question to that sentence, and one of the most elegant moves in the history of philosophy comes into view.

79
00:06:55.300 --> 00:07:00.230
The objection that appears in the dialogue is known as the turning of the tables.

80
00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:06.120
It works like this: Protagoras says that whatever seems true to a person is true for that person.

81
00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:10.250
Well then, what does the great majority of people in the world think?

82
00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:13.100
They think Protagoras is wrong.

83
00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:17.230
As they see it, truth does not change from person to person.

84
00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:27.280
Now Protagoras is obliged to apply his own rule: if whatever seems so to a person is true for that person, then their seeing that "Protagoras is wrong" is also true for them.

85
00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:33.170
Which means his doctrine, by the terms of its own rule, is forced to concede its own falsehood.

86
00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:36.180
This is no pious play on words.

87
00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:39.350
It is something we live through at the kitchen table.

88
00:07:39.350 --> 00:07:47.320
Someone, at the moment the argument heats up, says "everyone has their own truth," and lays it on the table like a peace offering.

89
00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:55.610
But notice what they are doing as they say it: they are declaring a rule that holds for everyone at that table, a rule that does not vary from person to person.

90
00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:59.100
They are doing precisely the thing they have just forbidden.

91
00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:10.910
And when the person across from them says "no, some things really are the case," they are forced to reply "you are wrong," even though by their own rule no one can be wrong.

92
00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:14.560
The second trouble with that sentence cuts deeper still.

93
00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:18.850
If everyone has a separate truth, then no argument means anything.

94
00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:30.980
And not only argument: apologizing becomes meaningless, because there is no longer any such fact as "I hurt you"; there is only your feeling that I did and my not feeling it, and the two carry equal weight.

95
00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:37.190
The courtroom becomes meaningless, because witnesses contradicting one another ceases to be a problem.

96
00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:45.640
Going to the doctor becomes meaningless, because there is no referee standing between the patient's opinion of themselves and the result of the test.

97
00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:51.370
And now go back to the story of the elephant, because there is something in it we tend to miss.

98
00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:56.460
The story gives us four different descriptions and says, look, no one knows the whole.

99
00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:59.470
But who is saying that?

100
00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:02.320
The person telling the story.

101
00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:05.650
That person knows there is an elephant in the room.

102
00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:09.300
They know the trunk is a trunk and the ear is an ear.

103
00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:15.510
Which means the story itself only makes sense if somewhere there really is an elephant and someone can see it whole.

104
00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:19.640
If there is no elephant, the four men cannot be mistaken.

105
00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:23.450
To be mistaken, there has to be something to be mistaken about.

106
00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:28.060
And this undercuts the very lesson people popularly draw from the tale.

107
00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:32.190
The fable of the elephant is not saying that there is no truth.

108
00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:37.760
It says the opposite: there is a truth, and our grasp of it is partial.

109
00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:40.610
These are not the same sentence.

110
00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:44.100
The first comforts us.

111
00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:46.790
The second gives us work to do.

112
00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:50.440
So what happens if we lose that distinction?

113
00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:58.730
Let us look at the moments when two great thinkers, seven hundred years and thousands of kilometers apart, felt the ground pulled out from under their feet.

114
00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:04.300
At the end of the nineteenth century, Friedrich Nietzsche jots a short note into his notebook.

115
00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:15.630
Objecting to positivism, he writes: they say "there are only facts"; no, facts are precisely what there is not, there are only interpretations.

116
00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:22.640
That line circulates today like graffiti on a wall, and in almost every argument it sits in someone's hand as a weapon.

117
00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:25.170
Two things are worth remembering.

118
00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:35.540
First, the line appears not in a book Nietzsche published but in notebook entries compiled after his death; the compilation known as The Will to Power is not a work he assembled himself,

119
00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:39.190
but a selection arranged by his sister and by editors.

120
00:10:39.190 --> 00:10:44.920
Second, and more importantly, Nietzsche does not leave the sentence sitting there and walk away.

121
00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:52.890
Immediately afterward, as though he knows exactly what his reader is about to say, he adds: and what if this too is an interpretation?

122
00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:56.380
You will be impatient to say so, will you not?

123
00:10:56.380 --> 00:10:58.910
Well then, so much the better.

124
00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:06.400
So the trap that was laid beneath Protagoras, Nietzsche lays with his own hand beneath his own sentence, and walks on smiling.

125
00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:08.610
It can look like an evasion.

126
00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:13.540
But in the book On the Genealogy of Morality he says what he means far more plainly.

127
00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:20.070
There he writes that seeing is only ever seeing from a perspective, and knowing only ever knowing from a perspective.

128
00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:30.600
And the sentence that follows is the whole point of this chapter: the more eyes, the more different eyes we can bring to bear on a thing, the more complete our concept of that thing will be.

129
00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:36.330
This is not "everyone has their own truth, let us drop the subject." It is the exact opposite.

130
00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:43.660
Nietzsche is saying that a single palm is not enough, and his solution is more hands, more contact, more looking.

131
00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:48.910
The limit of perspective is not an excuse for laziness; it is the reason to get to work.

132
00:11:48.910 --> 00:11:54.000
Now let us go back seven hundred years, to eleventh-century Khorasan.

133
00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:05.650
Abu Hamid al-Ghazali stands at the head of the Nizamiyya Madrasa in Baghdad; he teaches from the most prestigious chair in the Islamic world, his students number in the hundreds, his word carries weight.

134
00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:10.100
And in ten ninety-five, he falls apart.

135
00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:17.590
By his own account he loses the power of speech, becomes unable to lecture, his throat seizes up, he cannot eat.

136
00:12:17.590 --> 00:12:24.760
After a while he leaves Baghdad, gives away his wealth, and sets out on a withdrawal from the world that will last for years.

137
00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:32.410
He tells the story of this collapse himself, in the account of his life called al-Munqidh min al-Dalal, Deliverance from Error.

138
00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:38.780
And the path he takes as he describes what happened to him is exactly our dark room.

139
00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:41.150
First he doubts his senses.

140
00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:48.160
My eye shows me a star the size of a coin, he says; yet the calculations tell us it is larger than the earth.

141
00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:54.050
I look at a shadow and think it is standing still; I come back an hour later and it has moved.

142
00:12:54.050 --> 00:13:00.100
So the senses make me grasp one part of the elephant and say, this is all there is.

143
00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:04.870
But al-Ghazali does not stop there, and he opens the real abyss.

144
00:13:04.870 --> 00:13:08.360
He says: it was reason that showed me my senses had erred.

145
00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:11.690
But what if there is a judge above reason as well?

146
00:13:11.690 --> 00:13:23.500
Just as, when I am asleep, I take my dream to be entirely real and only understand my error upon waking, perhaps this waking state is itself a kind of sleep, and the true awakening comes after it.

147
00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:29.390
In that case, the things I now call self-evident may be things I will laugh at once I wake.

148
00:13:29.390 --> 00:13:36.240
This thought puts him, by his own account, into a state lasting roughly two months in which he could trust nothing at all.

149
00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:45.010
Note this well: it is not a thesis advanced in a student debate; it is a crisis that destroys the man's life, his work, and his health.

150
00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:48.980
And what did al-Ghazali draw from that crisis?

151
00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:52.790
He did not say, so everyone has their own truth.

152
00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:55.800
He did the exact opposite.

153
00:13:55.800 --> 00:14:04.090
He named the thing he was looking for: yaqin, certainty, a knowledge in which the thing known is laid open in a way that leaves no room for doubt.

154
00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:12.860
And he set the standard for it harshly: even if someone came and turned a stone into gold before my eyes, I should not fall into doubt about what I know.

155
00:14:12.860 --> 00:14:20.990
And when he regained that trust, he says he did not win it through a proof; in his own telling, through a light that God cast into his heart.

156
00:14:20.990 --> 00:14:24.480
Now set these two men side by side.

157
00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:27.010
Both lost their footing.

158
00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:30.340
Both saw that every gaze is a gaze from somewhere.

159
00:14:30.340 --> 00:14:33.350
But neither of them stayed sitting in that room.

160
00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:38.280
One said, more eyes; the other said, a deeper certainty.

161
00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:40.970
Both of them moved.

162
00:14:40.970 --> 00:14:47.500
Perhaps that is the moral of the story: noticing the darkness is not an arrival, it is a reason to set out.

163
00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:54.830
The trouble is that today there is a brand-new device that hands us this darkness, and that device does not want us to go outside at all.

164
00:14:54.830 --> 00:14:58.800
In Rumi's room, everyone was touching the same elephant.

165
00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:02.290
In today's room, we cannot even say that much.

166
00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:04.500
Think of the phone in your hand.

167
00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:09.750
Two friends sit side by side, open the same app, search the same word.

168
00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:13.080
What appears on their screens is not the same.

169
00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:20.250
The first person to state this claim with real clarity was Eli Pariser; in his book The Filter Bubble, published in twenty eleven,

170
00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:25.820
he described how search engines and social networks no longer show everyone the same page.

171
00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:36.030
In his well-known example, two acquaintances searched the name of the same country: one screen filled with street protests and political news, the other with holiday and travel suggestions.

172
00:15:36.030 --> 00:15:38.240
Nobody had lied.

173
00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:43.170
The system had simply given each of them more of whatever they had clicked on in the past.

174
00:15:43.170 --> 00:15:47.460
Let us take the picture The Filter Bubble describes one step further.

175
00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:53.190
In the dark room there were four men, and at the very least they could hear each other shouting.

176
00:15:53.190 --> 00:15:55.240
They could quarrel.

177
00:15:55.240 --> 00:16:01.450
Today each of us is in a room of our own, and the walls of that room are built out of our own past choices.

178
00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:05.900
What is more, that room is not dark; it is brilliantly lit.

179
00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:09.070
Inside, everything looks perfectly clear.

180
00:16:09.070 --> 00:16:20.080
Hundreds of confirmations arrive every day telling us that the piece in our hand is not a trunk but the whole elephant: likes, applause, comments saying the same thing, videos confirming the same thing.

181
00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:23.890
This is a worse condition than holding one part of the elephant.

182
00:16:23.890 --> 00:16:28.340
Because there is no friction left to show you that the piece in your hand is a piece.

183
00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:34.390
Here we have to be honest: researchers also say that this bubble is not as sealed as it is thought to be.

184
00:16:34.390 --> 00:16:39.320
Most people do still encounter content that runs against their own views.

185
00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:42.650
But what do they do when they encounter it?

186
00:16:42.650 --> 00:16:48.860
Usually they see it not as a description worth understanding, but as proof of the other side's bad faith.

187
00:16:48.860 --> 00:16:54.590
So the real problem may not lie in access to information, but in the reaction to the touch.

188
00:16:54.590 --> 00:17:04.160
Think of two people watching an election night in two different houses, or two generations in a family group chat telling the story of the same event in completely different ways.

189
00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:07.010
What the other person is saying is heard.

190
00:17:07.010 --> 00:17:10.020
It is simply not listened to.

191
00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:14.150
And this makes visible the real work the story leaves us.

192
00:17:14.150 --> 00:17:21.800
After listing the four men's descriptions, Rumi does not end the story by saying, this is the truth, and everyone has their own.

193
00:17:21.800 --> 00:17:29.130
His ending is this: if each of them had held a candle in his palm, this divergence in their words would have disappeared.

194
00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:32.460
The whole matter is in that single sentence.

195
00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:38.030
Rumi had turned blindness into darkness, remember, because darkness has a remedy.

196
00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:42.480
The candle does not change the fate of the gaze, but it changes the room.

197
00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:48.530
And the question of who is to light the candle is the one real responsibility this story hands down to us.

198
00:17:48.530 --> 00:17:51.380
So what is that candle?

199
00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:57.110
It is easy to say what it is not: nobody has a candle called "the full view" in their pocket.

200
00:17:57.110 --> 00:18:01.880
Nobody stands outside the room and sees the elephant whole.

201
00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:06.010
But the antidote to darkness does not have to be the sun.

202
00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:08.220
One candle is enough.

203
00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:12.510
And in this story, the candle is most often someone else's palm.

204
00:18:12.510 --> 00:18:20.000
When you ask "which years did you get?" instead of contradicting the father your sibling remembers, a candle is lit.

205
00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:27.810
When the testimonies of accident witnesses conflict, and instead of declaring one of them a liar you ask who was standing where, a candle is lit.

206
00:18:27.810 --> 00:18:35.300
When you wonder what kind of life stands behind that sentence on your screen, the one that makes your blood boil, a candle is lit.

207
00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:38.310
None of this shows you the elephant whole.

208
00:18:38.310 --> 00:18:43.240
It merely brightens the room a little, and shows you what it is you are actually holding.

209
00:18:43.240 --> 00:18:54.090
And let us keep this possibility honestly on the table too: when the candle is lit, seeing that what we hold is not an elephant but only an ear wounds our pride.

210
00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:57.580
Perhaps this is the saddest part of the story.

211
00:18:57.580 --> 00:19:01.870
We choose to remain in the dark, because in the dark everyone is right.

212
00:19:01.870 --> 00:19:07.280
In the light, somebody has to correct their description, and that somebody might be us.

213
00:19:07.280 --> 00:19:17.330
In the very first pages of the Masnavi, while describing the sound of the reed flute, Rumi says something like this: everyone became my friend according to their own assumptions,

214
00:19:17.330 --> 00:19:20.660
but no one went searching for the secrets within me.

215
00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:24.310
The story of the elephant is really the long version of that.

216
00:19:24.310 --> 00:19:33.240
We come to know the person before us, the event, the country, the book, by the measure of our own palm, and we give that measure the name truth.

217
00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:36.890
But the elephant is there.

218
00:19:36.890 --> 00:19:39.900
In the middle of the room, breathing.

219
00:19:39.900 --> 00:19:47.710
If everyone's truth were separate, there would be no point in correcting one another, in wondering, in asking, or even in arguing.

220
00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:52.640
The fact that we argue at all shows that deep down we all know there is an elephant.

221
00:19:52.640 --> 00:19:55.170
The quarrel is a sign of hope.

222
00:19:55.170 --> 00:20:00.100
As long as, in the middle of the quarrel, it occurs to someone to light the candle.

223
00:20:00.100 --> 00:20:04.230
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

224
00:20:04.230 --> 00:20:07.400
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
